# German changes what it is handed. A voiced obstruent loses its voice at the
# end of a syllable, so that a d written at the end of a word is said as a t;
# an r after a vowel becomes a vowel. That is German, and a module that is to
# say another language's phones has to say them as they were given: the
# language that was read has done its own phonology already, and doing
# German's on top of it is what a German accent is.
#
# Measured, with engine/accent/chassis.py in OpenEvv-sapi5 (github.com/joshknnd1982/OpenEvv-sapi5). German says b d g v z and its
# voiced affricate as p t k f s and the voiceless one at the end of a word and
# before a consonant, and r as the vowel R in both places. With this rule in
# place of IBM's, all of those are said as they are written, and everything
# else is said as German says it: an affricate is still a stop and a
# fricative, which is done elsewhere.
rule make_ger_phon_adjustments takes 1 from gt_phnol.obj
  afresh
  call ZZfence_null
  match
end
